## **4.2 REQUIREMENTS FOR DURABILITY**

### **4.2.1 General**

One of the main characteristics influencing the durability of concrete is its permeability to the ingress of water, oxygen, carbon dioxide and other potentially deleterious substances. Permeability is governed by the constituents and procedures used in making the concrete. With normal-weight aggregates a suitably low permeability is achieved by having an adequate cementitious content, a sufficiently low free water/cement ratio, complete compaction of the concrete, and sufficient hydration of the cement through proper curing.

The factors influencing durability include:

- (a) the shape and bulk of the concrete (clause 4.2.2.1);
- (b) the cover to embedded steel (clauses 4.2.2.2 and 4.2.4);

